{
  // LiveWire's app code. See README, "TypeScript", for why the settings are
  // where they are and what the plan is for tightening them.
  //
  // NOT THE FINISHED STATE, BUT MOST OF THE WAY. The first pass changed the
  // toolchain without changing the app: same code, same behaviour, emitted
  // JavaScript that matched what was already live. The real checks have been
  // turned on since one at a time, each in its own pass with the tests green
  // after it, because switching them all on at once would have buried the
  // change in a diff nobody could review.
  //
  // The flags, in the order they are worth doing:
  //   strictNullChecks             ON - was 524 findings, all resolved
  //   noImplicitAny                ON - was 984 findings, all resolved
  //   noUncheckedIndexedAccess     in progress - 200 found, 88 left
  //   + the rest of strict, unused 115 running total, catch variables mostly
  //
  // They do not add up separately - each changes what the next can infer - and
  // noUncheckedIndexedAccess reports nothing without strictNullChecks. See
  // README, "TypeScript", for the same table with the details.
  "compilerOptions": {
    // The app targets phone browsers from the last few years. Nothing here is
    // downlevelled, which is what keeps the emitted file readable and close to
    // what was written - a build whose output you cannot recognise is a build
    // you cannot debug from a phone screenshot.
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": ["ES2020", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],

    // NOT modules, and this is load-bearing rather than a preference.
    //
    // livewire.js is one classic script: 518 top-level declarations sharing one
    // global scope, no import or export anywhere. Two things depend on that.
    // The page loads boot-guard before it and relies on both being ordinary
    // scripts that run in order. And test/events.test.mjs appends a block
    // referring to those globals by bare name and runs the whole file through
    // `new Function()`, which cannot execute module syntax at all.
    //
    // Emitting modules would break the test harness and the global scope in the
    // same stroke, and buy nothing this app has a use for.
    //
    // What actually makes this a global script is that no file has a top-level
    // import or export - TypeScript treats such a file as a script, not a
    // module, and emits it as-is. This setting only says how module syntax
    // WOULD be emitted, and there is none to emit. ("none" was valid until
    // TypeScript 7 removed it.) test/lint.mjs fails if an import appears.
    "module": "es2020",

    // Written in src/, served from app/. The page and the service worker both
    // name /app/livewire.js, so the output path is fixed by them.
    "rootDir": "src",
    "outDir": "app",

    // The comments ARE the documentation here, and they shipped inside the
    // inline script for years. Stripping them would make the served file
    // useless to read.
    "removeComments": false,
    // .gitattributes pins LF. The compiler should not undo that.
    "newLine": "lf",
    // Nothing from @types is wanted. This app has one dependency and it is the
    // compiler itself.
    "types": [],

    // --- the permissive half, all of it temporary ---------------------------
    // TypeScript 7 always emits "use strict" - alwaysStrict:false was removed
    // from the compiler, so this is not a choice any more, it is a consequence.
    // It was checked rather than assumed. Strict mode would change this app if
    // it used `this` in a plain call, `arguments`, or an octal literal, and it
    // uses none of the three (0, 0 and 0 across both files, counted with
    // comments and strings blanked out). Everything else strict mode forbids -
    // assigning to an undeclared name, a duplicate parameter, `with` - is
    // already a compile error, and the compile is clean.
    //
    // And the tests exercise it, every run. The compiled file opens with
    // "use strict", and test/events.test.mjs passes that file as the BODY of
    // `new Function()` - where a directive at the top applies. So every
    // behaviour test runs the app strict, exactly as a phone does. (This comment
    // once said the opposite. It was wrong: it described `new Function()` in
    // general and missed that the directive arrives inside the body.)
    "strict": false,

    // ON. The first of the real checks, and the one that asks the question most
    // worth asking of this app: does the code check a thing is there before it
    // uses it? 524 findings when first switched on, every one resolved rather
    // than silenced - see README, "TypeScript".
    "strictNullChecks": true,

    // ON. Every value has a type the compiler can check, not one it had to
    // assume. 984 findings when first measured, typed an area at a time - the
    // data layer, anglers and catches, the director's tools, then everything
    // else - and switched on once there was nothing left for it to find.
    //
    // It cannot see an `any` somebody WROTE, which is how a value goes back to
    // having no shape without anyone noticing. test/lint.mjs does that half: a
    // hand-written `any` is a finding, apart from a short list of things that
    // come from outside and ship no types of their own.
    "noImplicitAny": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "types/**/*.d.ts"]
}
